    Pass in the card reader
    
    Instead of handing buyCoffee() the bank's answer, hand it the thing
    that gives answers. buyCoffee() keeps its job, charging the price and
    reacting, and whoever calls it decides which card reader it uses.
    
    That needs a type for "a card reader", so reader.ts declares IReader,
    with its contract: true if charged, false if declined, never throws.
    Before, cart.ts imported bank.ts: the cart depended on the bank. Now
    neither imports the other. The cart depends on IReader, and
    BankCardReader implements it. That is the dependency inversion
    principle. main.ts is the one place that picks the real bank.
    
    The tests hand buyCoffee() stand-ins for the bank, test doubles that
    always give the same answer, so each test picks the outcome it needs.
    Every test passes every run.

